{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>that we saw the distress of his soul when he begged us</b>  They considered the cruelty a greater punishment than the selling itself, for their brother of their own flesh was pleading and throwing himself down before them and they did not pity him. And Scripture did not recount this there, either because the matter is known by nature — that a person pleads to his brothers when they set out to harm him, and implores them by the life of their father, and does everything in his power to save himself from death — or because Scripture wished to abbreviate in its narrative flow, or following the way of the Scriptures, which abbreviate in one place and elaborate in another. Now, Reuben answered them: I already told you at the time of the deed not to sin against him, for he is a child, and because of his youth he sinned against you, and it is fitting for you to pass over the sins of his youth — and now his blood too, along with the cruelty that you are acknowledging, is being demanded. Alternatively, the meaning of \"also his blood\" may be: even though you did not kill him, HaShem will demand his blood from you, and it will be reckoned to you as though you had shed his blood, since he was sold into permanent servitude — for it is possible that he died on account of being a pampered child who never trained to labor. And our Sages interpreted (Genesis Rabbah 91:8): his blood, and the blood of the old man [i.e., Jacob their father]."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ב\"ר is resolved as בראשית רבה (Genesis Rabbah)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:04.907440793Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}